RANKING: B > C > E > D > A

B is most clearly written for an adult: fatigue, guarded conversation, social pressure and the gift of bread all carry meanings beyond their literal vocabulary. A is most clearly not. Its counting prompt, colour drills, sound effects, praise, explicit friendship moral and “colorín colorado” frame unmistakably address a young child.

B is the strongest because it turns one ordinary object—the extra bread—into an act of evasion, warning and attempted protection. None of the others makes such simple vocabulary carry so much unstated meaning.

D is the weakest because virtually nothing causes anything else. Carlos and his surroundings are catalogued category by category, so even his late wish to travel feels like another vocabulary item rather than a motivating desire. A is more insulting to the intended adult, but D is the more lifeless piece of writing.

Yes. I would pay for and finish a book written consistently at B’s level; C also shows enough intelligence to sustain interest. E is promising but needs grammatical editing. B and C are locally satisfying scenes within a larger mystery rather than fully independent whole stories, so the absolute claim in the third debate remains unresolved.

